{"kind":"report","text":"Approve the B2 offline/synchronization DESIGN document at 3bf379968741085d58b7ca9b61e844e07ef69be5, tree 2eec0b9caad36ddc4b952e628cdcc9f8bb40b766, against parent 3b468cd04c5cd3925592fed28d9e801323c92702. Primary d29e4a12 is the sole changed path, notes/2026-10-09-offline-and-sync-design.md. Normal Git-bound document review under original request 47923460 and builder promise 4950af44; no evidence-only, protocol adoption, implementation dispatch or Source enablement.\n\nThe note covers the offline and generic optimistic model requested in B2/R5 items 2 and 3, in plain English without product comparisons. Confirmed history, held observations, drafts, exact signed envelopes, local private outbox and tentative presentation have separate meanings. Only accepted scope entries change confirmed state. Application prediction grants no authority, capacity or settlement. Item revisions are not room revisions; each scope has its own incarnation, definition, head and cursor, with no fabricated atomic room head. Dependent drafts wait for accepted real IDs before signing.\n\nThe crucial recovery rules are correct as design. Durable envelope/accompanying bytes precede the attempt marker, which precedes transport. A crash after that marker is conservatively attempted/unknown even without an observed send. Never-attempted expiry is separate. An eligible retry submits the same envelope; a later refusal, absence at one head, elapsed device time, clock changes, inventory, retry delay or session failure does not settle a possible earlier acceptance or outside effect. Refusal consumes no key and is not cancellation. Attempted replacement needs exact accepted evidence or terminal absence at the target, original sender exclusion and preparation accounted for across involved scopes. Accepted receipt completes the old request and authorizes no duplicate effect; undo needs another authorized act. Draft revision and the person's replacement approval follow recovery rather than conceal uncertainty. A fresh signature or idempotency key never hides a mismatch.\n\nThe cited normative sections support those distinctions. Contract revision 23 sections 2.1, 4.2 and 9.1 fix canonical request identity, accepted-retry receipt behavior, refusal versus unavailable, position-specific absence and recorded terminal bases with preparation. The scope's unrecorded clock is not terminal evidence; an expiry checkpoint still follows ordinary drain/capacity rules. Authority revision 28 sections 3.9 and 5.4 keep read sessions nonsigning, clock/revocation windows qualified, duty-before-send and unknown effects settled only by their own authenticated answer or actual completion fence. Preparation absence in one scope proves nothing about another. Root directly read all of 9.1/3.9/5.4 and current read.ts; an independent reader read all five exact sections. This is selected-section credit, not a fresh review/adoption of the entire large contract or authority text.\n\nThe I3 fourth-milestone delivery explicitly names adopted revision 23 at 3b3e394 and revision 28 at 8b1c3c9 as its historical bases. Root safely inspected artifact records e15527 and d8e94 and their exact commits/paths. Those are artifact IDs, not adoption events. The historical note and standing I3 landing evidence support attribution; its old gates, labelled host/scripted entries, incomplete dimensions and unresolved replay/runtime/provider duties are not renewed as current proof.\n\nThe actual API gap is explicit and independently confirmed: current Settlement = Read\u003cReceipt\u003e carries no terminal basis or preparation status. The first slice must close and witness the API/runtime/client gap before automatic replacement of attempted work. Choosing one existing act without outside preparation sequences the full goal; it does not supply terminal evidence by itself. Actual act/storage/locking/durability/retention/loss contracts and interruption/competing-sender witnesses remain prerequisites. No offline implementation or those witnesses ran during review.\n\nOne device keeps one key and its private outbox. Another device's accepted-history access neither copies the key nor recovers lost private drafts, envelopes or credentials. A background sender submits existing signatures; a read session does not sign or grant authority. Revocation, missing history and renewal failure retain historical/unknown custody honestly, with the existing clock/window assumptions. Public projection/export/diagnostics exclude secrets. Storage loss, logout, erasure, retention budgets and authorized recovery require real custody outcomes; preserving unresolved records is no promise of recovery after loss.\n\nPack claims are supported by fresh static transport source reads: configured safe-integer maxBytes, command framing plus pack in the POST allowance, and separate wire, inflated, constructed/cache, count and per-object limits. Those buffers and copies can coexist. This is not a single aggregate peak-memory, global concurrency, CPU, platform-capacity or durable-retention ceiling. No arbitrary-pack room upload act, reachability/publication proof or split protocol is invented. Future route/domain/object closure, authentication, reservation, custody, retention, expiry, recovery and measured limits remain owner-approved delivery duties. Text-manifest work proves no pack feature. Local limits stop new admission instead of evicting unknown requests or secrets. Full multi-scope/dependent drafts, long-disconnection/incomplete-history, outside-duty recovery, generic optimistic applications and requested pack functionality remain owed; the stages and after-14th plan do not reduce scope. This review closes none of R5's other items or Gate 2.\n\nCoverage and native audit pass with exit 0: Root and peer freshly read the whole 303-line BODY and entire native U3; normative peer independently read the BODY. Two unique design units total 42,472 bytes; five independent design units total 105,912 bytes in 17 bounded displays. Five exact normative sections total 37,695 bytes; six supporting bodies total 77,830 bytes. All four sealed reports were read fully by Root. Native identities, slice hashes/offsets/EOF, canonical artifact bindings and source/package identity were checked without prior/equal-body shortcuts. AGGREGATE.json SHA256 d6dd228c8334c4d46d1b8d46c7867f95efb0c59b53d048a3978be3739199b9ea.\n\nOnly this document changes. Candidate source/tests/scripts/manifest/lock equal the stated 3b468 baseline; native whitespace check passes. No new tests, gate, installation, project execution, probes, generated inputs, provider/device/browser/private-configuration or raw-envelope/signature/payload access. The retained current-source gate remains separately qualified by its runtime diagnostic, owner risk decision and outstanding A4 work. Normal landing must preserve the latest main, including GitHub 6afa, sprint report 1e9 and accepted v2 documents 4ab8f849, plus later changes. This is approval to publish the exact design proposal, with normal receipt/succession owed, not self-ratification or checker landing.\n","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d29e4a12c8d6b8b06e49244b9861e3521439c867","binding":"assigned","head":"3bf379968741085d58b7ca9b61e844e07ef69be5","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f52d9b4eeda9ab0a3e250cbd98a3289638de287d\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4950af44f65b6cb4ccfeeece35ec758910be0597\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c864bb2110fbf09c7e0545d60b849ab1958b4dbf","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}